From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jayden Milne <linux@jayd.ml>
Date: Sun, 1 Nov 2026 20:04:13 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] iio: qcom_smgr: give the gyroscope and magnetometer a
 rotation matrix

All 3-axis sensors share one mount matrix, [0, -1, 0; -1, 0, 0; 0, 0, 1].
On the Galaxy Tab S3, SMGR reports them in north-east-down coordinates: X
towards the top edge in portrait, Y to the right and Z into the screen.
The rotation into the device frame of the mount-matrix binding (X right,
Y top, Z out of the screen) is [0, 1, 0; 1, 0, 0; 0, 0, -1]. The shared
matrix is its negation, which is not a rotation and flips the sign of
every axis.

For the gyroscope and the magnetometer that inverts every reading: a
compass heading from the magnetometer pointed south when the device
pointed north, and a counter-clockwise turn of the device lying face up
gave a negative angular velocity around Z. Give them the rotation.

Leave the accelerometer's matrix alone. SMGR reports +1 g upwards and
iio-sensor-proxy takes a negative Y as upright, so its X and Y signs are
what screen rotation on a board without a matrix of its own relies on.
Its Z is not what iio-sensor-proxy's tilt expects (lying face up reads as
face down), which this patch does not change.

The other boards this driver runs on have not been checked: the axes SMGR
reports may depend on the board.

The driver is in the msm8996-mainline tree (v6.19.5-msm8996), not in
mainline yet.

Tested on a Galaxy Tab S3 (SM-T820) lying flat, with the shared matrix: a
heading from the magnetometer's X and Y read 180 degrees when a phone
compass said north and 270 at east, and counter-clockwise turns read
about -0.6 rad/s on the gyroscope's Z. The rotation negates all three, so
north comes to 0, east to 90 and the turn to a positive value. With this
patch in_mount_matrix reads the rotation for both sensors and the
accelerometer's is unchanged. The readings were not repeated with the
patch, and the magnetometer's Z and the gyroscope's X and Y were not
checked.

Signed-off-by: Jayden Milne <linux@jayd.ml>
Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
---
 drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c | 23 ++++++++++++++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 22 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c b/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
index 248215257c..4cb9c9926b 100644
--- a/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
+++ b/drivers/iio/common/qcom_smgr/qcom_smgr.c
@@ -398,8 +398,26 @@ static const struct iio_buffer_setup_ops qcom_smgr_buffer_ops = {
 	.predisable = &qcom_smgr_sensor_predisable
 };
 
-/* SMGR reports values for 3-axis sensors in north-east-down coordinates */
+/*
+ * SMGR reports values for 3-axis sensors in north-east-down coordinates:
+ * X towards the top of the screen, Y towards its right side and Z into it.
+ * This rotation turns them into the usual device frame: X right, Y top,
+ * Z out of the screen.
+ */
 static const struct iio_mount_matrix qcom_smgr_iio_mount_matrix = {
+	.rotation = {
+		"0", "1", "0",
+		"1", "0", "0",
+		"0", "0", "-1"
+	}
+};
+
+/*
+ * SMGR reports +1 g upwards at rest, while iio-sensor-proxy takes a negative
+ * Y as the upright orientation. The matrix the accelerometer has always had
+ * negates the rotation above to give that, which is not a rotation itself.
+ */
+static const struct iio_mount_matrix qcom_smgr_iio_accel_mount_matrix = {
 	.rotation = {
 		"0", "-1", "0",
 		"-1", "0", "0",
@@ -411,6 +429,9 @@ static const struct iio_mount_matrix *
 qcom_smgr_iio_get_mount_matrix(const struct iio_dev *iio_dev,
 			       const struct iio_chan_spec *chan)
 {
+	if (chan->type == IIO_ACCEL)
+		return &qcom_smgr_iio_accel_mount_matrix;
+
 	return &qcom_smgr_iio_mount_matrix;
 }
 
